    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d515-a1">

                <pb facs="0878" n="808" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg808"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 68.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Byers to Mr.
                            Cramer.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Zurich</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-07-04"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 4,
                        1883</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Allow me to call your attention to the case
                    of five deaf and dumb emigrants who went to the United States some time since
                    from an asylum in the <hi rendition="#italic">gemeinde</hi> of Walkringen, in
                    canton Berne. A sixth deaf and dumb person from the same institution called here
                    to-day, saying he had 60 francs in money, and his <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >gemeinde</hi> or township was about to aid him off to America. I advised
                    him not to go. He said his five comrades are doing well in America, and that
                    Consul Mason, in Basel, gave them certificates at the time that there was no
                    hindrance to their emigrating to the United States. I scarcely think this
                    statement probable. It seems astounding that any community should permit an
                    asylum within its borders to empty its inmates into a foreign country. Not one
                    of them, it is said, could speak or hear a word. My only information is from the
                    mute who calls here. Trusting the matter will be investigated.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>S. H. M. BYERS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
